title The counter derived from its own result
prompt patchouli seat, autonomous lap -- the card's MINE line: merge and annotate reach the two counters through @max alone and state no postcondition of their own
rota lap 4983, row 3 -- Water tastes; read up close and run the actual thing rather than argue it. The row's cardinal seat is what-brix-infuse-is, whose test is running the infusion a SECOND time. That is what this lap did to its own plant: a @min tasted right and was wrong, and only running it against the elder claims showed it reds for the general law rather than for the gap. The second run is what separated the plant that isolates from the plant that merely fires.
think Read Weave.merge. It sets next_pos and next_run to @max of the two sides, then walks the merged LINES asserting each stands below those numbers. That walk is a reading of the outputs.
think Asked when the walk can be fooled. It is exactly as tight as the tightest side: apply, planted, wide_weave, from_v1 and the witness's own seeded all build weaves standing one above their highest line, so a fallen counter is caught by a line that reaches it.
think V2Record's own head already names the exception, three hundred lines up: the record DECLINES to inherit the tightness, and from_v2 refuses only a counter at or below a row it holds. So a restored weave may declare next_pos 5 while holding position 2.
think First plant was @min on next_pos. Ran it against the module with the four new asserts deleted AND with the new claim deleted: exit 134 both ways. The elder claims catch it, so it proves the max's general law rather than this gap. Wrong plant.
think Second plant derives next_pos from the result's lines -- the exact fault V2Record warns about. It EQUALS the max on every tight weave. With claim 11 removed: exit 0, every elder claim passing. With claim 11 present: 134. Isolating, shown from both sides.
obs Two of the four asserts I first wrote on Note.gen were tautologies of the @max one line above them, so the compiler folds them and a reader learns the ones beside them might not fire either. Taken out the same lap; the two kept are derived rather than restated.
obs The four counter asserts in merge are honestly a restatement -- claim 11 reds with them deleted, measured. What they buy is the reading for every caller rather than for one witness input. The lift's run assert one constructor over is named the same way, and that precedent is why the comment says so out loud.
obs plant_apply refused the annotate control's derive plant by name: Note.gen's new binding retired the literal that sed had carried. REDS %519 doing its whole job -- a plant that matches nothing reads exactly like a law that holds.
obs THE COLD BASELINE IS INVALID BY MY OWN HAND. I launched the cold endurance run and then edited the tree while it ran, against the baton's hold-still clause. The hot run after staging is the binding reading, and the cold pass is reported rather than relied on.
obs The REDS pin had 2,804 bytes of headroom and the row needed more. Trimmed twice, then folded %739 onto a shelf.
obs THE SEND'S REBASE UNDID BOTH. Six upstream rows landed while the lap ran, so %743 was re-booked %750, and the push's own spine gate then refused THAT as squatted too, at 20260915.202715 -- re-booked %751; the key stayed the stamp. And upstream had already folded %739 to its own shelf at 20260915.215554, an earlier stamp: my shelf was a second home for one row, which is a red rather than a repair, so it was withdrawn whole and upstream's REDS.md and fold recital taken as they stood.
obs %751 is BORN on its shelf instead, the pin reading verdict=over_bound_foldable at 211 bytes over with the row in it. A row that is CLOSED the moment it lands has no reason to sit on the pin.
